<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arnulfo Arias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arnulfo Arias was a Panamanian physician and nationalist politician who was elected President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> three times and removed by force each time. His first term brought a new constitution in 1941 and measures against foreign minorities before he was deposed later that year; he was removed again in 1951. Elected in 1968, he was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> after eleven days, opening the military era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-torrijos">Omar Torrijos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arnulfo-arias.jpg" alt="Arnulfo Arias" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of State. U.S. Embassy, Panama. 4/3/1964 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A._Arias_President_of_Panama_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arnulfo Arias Madrid was born on 15 August 1901 in Penonomé, in Coclé Province, the son of Antonio Arias and Carmen Madrid and brother of Harmodio Arias, who also served as president. He studied at the French Christian Brothership, today La Salle, in his native town, attended secondary school in New York City and studied medicine and surgery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>, specialising in psychiatry, obstetrics and endocrinology. In 1925 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and took the leadership of the nationalist organisation Patriotic Communal Action, which drew on discontent with the influence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and became the nucleus of the present Panameñista Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1931 Arias led a coup that deposed President Florencio Harmodio Arosemena, and in the following year helped his brother Harmodio Arias to the presidency. He later served in the cabinet and as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>.</p><p>Elected president in 1940 by a majority greater than any before, he enacted a new constitution that gave women the vote for the first time. Because of his post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> some claimed he was pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, and some historians think Roosevelt supported his removal. He was ousted in October 1941 in a coup engineered by the police. Critics described his tenure as marked by corruption, nepotism and autocratic practices.</p><p>He clearly won the election of 1948 but was not seated, and the National Assembly declared him the winner a year later. He was removed again in 1951. Elected in 1968 at the head of a five-party coalition, he took office in October, tried to restructure the command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> and after eleven days was ousted for the third time, escaping to the Canal Zone and then to Miami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arias returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> in 1978 and ran again in 1984, at the age of 83. When exit polls showed him well ahead, the government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-noriega">Manuel Noriega</a> halted the count, and Nicolás Ardito Barletta was declared the winner by 1,713 votes; independent observers estimated that Arias would have won in a landslide in a fair election.</p><p>Arias died of a heart attack at his home in Coral Gables, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, on 10 August 1988, and his death caused national mourning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>. His supporters protested against Noriega at his funeral. His remains were exhumed on 8 January 2012 and he was reburied with a state funeral in a mausoleum near the Arias Madrid Family Museum in Penonomé. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mireya-moscoso">Mireya Moscoso</a> in 1969, who later became the first woman president of Panama.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arnulfo Arias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The nationalist <b>Arnulfo Arias</b> held the presidency in 1940–1941, 1949–1951 and 1968 and was removed each time, the last only eleven days after taking office, when the National Guard seized power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Penonomé, in what is now <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a>. Died in Coral Gables, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=8.52,-80.36&amp;year=1957" aria-label="Penonomé on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2330 1845 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arnulfo Arias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2780 2115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2780 2115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=8.52,-80.36&amp;year=1957">What was here in 1957? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
